{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 6, "model_name": "meta/llama-4-maverick-17b-128e-instruct", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a nobleman and leader of the County of Tripoli a Crusader state in the Holy Land. He was born around 1140 and died in 1187. Raymond was the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem. After his father's assassination in 1152 *Early Life and Regency* Raymond II's widow Hodierna took charge and ruled Tripoli as regent for a short period before handing over power to her son.\n\nRaymond III was taken captive by Nur ad-Din Zangi in 1164 during the Battle of Harim and was held prisoner for nearly a decade until his release in 1174. During his captivity *Reign and Military Campaigns* the regency of Tripoli was held by Amalric I the King of Jerusalem. After his release Raymond played a significant role in the politics of the Crusader states and was a key figure in the succession crisis that followed the death of King Amalric in 1174.\n\nRaymond III was a strong supporter of the Ibelin family and opposed the rise of Guy of Lusignan. He was also a key player in the events leading up to the *Battle of Hattin and Death* where he commanded the left flank of the Crusader army. The battle was a disastrous defeat for the Crusaders and Raymond's own forces suffered heavy casualties. Raymond himself escaped the battlefield and fled to Tyre where he died soon after of pleurisy.\n\nThroughout his life Raymond III was known for his military prowess and his complex and often tumultuous relationships with other Crusader leaders. His reign was marked by a series of conflicts with neighboring Muslim states and power struggles within the Crusader community. Despite his many challenges Raymond remained a significant figure in the history of the Crusades and his legacy continued to shape the politics of the Holy Land for generations to come.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.011065074242651463}, {"token":